Freestanding libc declarations for harvesting the real RISC-V PoCs.

`riscv64-elf-gcc` (Homebrew) is a bare-metal toolchain with no newlib headers,
and Apple clang has no riscv64 backend, so the upstream PoCs cannot be compiled
as-is on macOS. spec/harvest_real_riscv.py only ever runs the compiler with -S
and never links, so declarations suffice.

This does not perturb the code we harvest. The gadget functions call no libc at
all -- they are array indexing, a bounds check, inline asm and a probe load. The
libc references live in the harness (printf/memset/random), which affects only
whether a call target resolves at link time, never the instructions GCC emits
for the gadget. The one place it could matter -- glibc's headers sometimes
provide builtins that change codegen -- does not arise, because no gadget
function calls any of the stubbed functions.

stdint.h forwards to gcc's self-contained <stdint-gcc.h>: gcc's own <stdint.h>
does `#include_next <stdint.h>` expecting a libc one that is not present.

Security-RISC/spectre.c additionally needs -std=gnu17. It calls rdcycle(&junk2)
against a 0-argument declaration -- legal before C23, where `()` meant
"unspecified arguments". GCC 16 defaults to C23, where `()` means `(void)`, so
it errors. Compiling as gnu17 matches the toolchain the authors used rather
than editing their source.
